Chapter: ZstdTest
  cites Compress chapter Zstd

 Compress, decompress, verify byte identity -- and verify the output is SMALLER
 on run-heavy input, the assertion a pass-through cannot pass.

 zstd-compress emitted only raw blocks and inflated everything it was given.
 RLE blocks (Block_Type 01) now give real compression on runs. A round-trip
 alone cannot tell a compressor from a pipe, so the size assertion runs on a
 compressible input: two long runs. The mixed input exercises the raw-block
 fallback and the RLE/raw boundary in one round-trip.

Section: Helpers

  list-equal : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  list-equal (a) (b) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then True
    else if list-at a i == list-at b i then list-equal a b (i + 1) len
    else False

  check-roundtrip : List Integer, Text -> Text
  check-roundtrip (input) (label) =
    let compressed = zstd-compress input
    in let decompressed = zstd-decompress compressed
    in let ok = if list-length decompressed == list-length input then list-equal input decompressed 0 (list-length input) else False
    in label & "=" & if ok then "pass" else "fail"

  check-smaller : List Integer, Text -> Text
  check-smaller (input) (label) =
    let compressed = zstd-compress input
    in label & "=" & if list-length compressed < list-length input then "pass" else "fail"

  run-of : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  run-of (v) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else run-of v n (i + 1) (list-push acc v)

Section: Body

  test-empty : List Integer = []
  test-small : List Integer = [72, 101, 108, 108, 111]

 Two runs of three hundred bytes: long enough that the nine-byte frame does
 not swamp the win, and pure runs are exactly what RLE blocks encode.

  test-runs : List Integer = run-of 66 300 0 (run-of 65 300 0 [])

 Literals, then a run, then literals: exercises raw fallback and both block
 boundaries in a single round-trip.

  test-mixed : List Integer = [1, 2, 3] & run-of 9 20 0 [] & [4, 5, 6]

 A skewed eight-byte cycle over four symbols with no byte repeated even twice in
 a row, so RLE never fires and the whole thing is one literal region that becomes
 a Huffman compressed block. zstd-huf-smaller is the assertion RLE-and-raw-only
 Zstd fails, and the round-trip drives our own decoder through the compressed
 path (real zstd drives it through build/zstd-interop-test.ps1).

  nr-byte : Integer -> Integer
  nr-byte (i) = list-at [65, 66, 65, 67, 65, 66, 65, 68] (i - (i / 8) * 8)

  gen-nr : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gen-nr (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else gen-nr n (i + 1) (list-push acc (nr-byte i))

  test-huf : List Integer = gen-nr 320 0 []

 A second no-run cycle over symbols 10, 40 and 71, so the highest byte is odd and
 the weight header ends on a half-used byte -- the odd-nibble path our decoder
 must skip correctly.

  nr2-byte : Integer -> Integer
  nr2-byte (i) = list-at [10, 71, 10, 40, 10, 71, 10, 40, 71, 40, 71, 10] (i - (i / 12) * 12)

  gen-nr2 : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gen-nr2 (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else gen-nr2 n (i + 1) (list-push acc (nr2-byte i))

  test-huf2 : List Integer = gen-nr2 300 0 []

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (check-roundtrip test-empty "zstd-empty")
    print-line-uni (check-roundtrip test-small "zstd-small")
    print-line-uni (check-roundtrip test-runs "zstd-runs")
    print-line-uni (check-roundtrip test-mixed "zstd-mixed")
    print-line-uni (check-roundtrip test-huf "zstd-huf")
    print-line-uni (check-roundtrip test-huf2 "zstd-huf2")
    print-line-uni (check-smaller test-runs "zstd-smaller")
    print-line-uni (check-smaller test-huf "zstd-huf-smaller")
    print-line-uni ("zstd-valid=" & if zstd-valid (zstd-compress test-small) then "pass" else "fail")
    print-line-uni ("zstd-invalid=" & if zstd-valid [0, 0, 0] then "fail" else "pass")
  end
